# =============================================================================
# docker/.env.example - TEMPLATE for the values docker/docker-compose.yml
#                       interpolates when it starts the container topology.
# =============================================================================
#
# WHAT THIS FILE IS
#   The documented contract for every variable the compose file substitutes at
#   run time. It is committed to source control, so it holds NO REAL VALUES:
#   no server, no database credentials, no signing key. Every value below is a
#   placeholder chosen to fail loudly if it is deployed unchanged, rather than
#   to look plausible.
#
# HOW TO USE IT
#       cp docker/.env.example docker/.env      # then edit docker/.env
#
#   Compose reads `.env` from the directory that CONTAINS docker-compose.yml,
#   so the file has to be docker/.env - a .env at the repository root is not
#   read. Exporting the same names in the invoking shell works identically and
#   takes precedence, which is how a real deployment should supply them: from
#   the orchestrator's secret store, never from a file on disk.
#
# NEVER COMMIT docker/.env
#   It holds real credentials. The repository's .gitignore already enforces
#   that with `.env*` and `*.env` patterns plus a `!**/.env.example` negation
#   that keeps THIS file - and only this file - tracked. Confirm both halves
#   before you commit anything:
#       git check-ignore -v docker/.env           # expect a match: ignored
#       git check-ignore -v docker/.env.example   # expect no match: tracked
#
# WHY THAT RULE IS ABSOLUTE IN THIS REPOSITORY
#   The Web Forms application this API replaces committed the 3DES key that
#   decrypted every stored user password directly into Website/release.config,
#   in plain sight, while storing those passwords reversibly rather than
#   hashing them. A single committed key therefore laid open every credential
#   in the database. Ending that arrangement is one of the goals of this
#   migration, so no secret - not a real one, not a convincing sample - is
#   ever written into a tracked file.
#
# THERE IS NO DATABASE CONTAINER
#   docker-compose.yml declares two services, `api` and `frontend`. SQL Server
#   is always EXTERNAL to the topology and must already contain the existing
#   DotNetNuke schema: the API maps onto that schema and never creates, alters
#   or drops any part of it. Nothing here provisions a database.
#
# FILE FORMAT
#   KEY=value, one entry per line, no spaces around `=`, no `export` prefix,
#   no quotes and no line continuations; `#` begins a comment. Do not quote a
#   value - Compose keeps the quote characters and they become part of the
#   value. Semicolons need no escaping, because this is not a shell script and
#   Compose does not word-split the line.
#
# WHAT THIS ARRANGEMENT DOES NOT PROTECT AGAINST, STATED PLAINLY
#   Every value below reaches the API as an ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE, because that is
#   what the compose file the migration plan preserves (AAP 0.9.3) declares:
#   `ConnectionStrings__Default=${DB_CONNECTION_STRING}` and
#   `Jwt__Secret=${JWT_SECRET}`. An environment variable is readable from the
#   container's own metadata, so ANY user or process that can reach the Docker
#   socket can recover both values from a RUNNING container:
#       docker inspect dnnmigration-api | grep Jwt__Secret
#   Runtime testing confirmed that this is the ONLY surface that exposes them.
#   Neither value appears in either image's history, in its layers, in its
#   configuration, anywhere on the API image's filesystem, in the served frontend
#   bundle, or in either container's logs - all measured at zero occurrences,
#   including for an issued access token. The exposure is confined to live-container
#   inspection, and it follows from the frozen example rather than from a choice
#   made here.
#
#   FOR A DEPLOYMENT THAT CANNOT ACCEPT THAT, the values move without any code
#   change, because the API reads ordinary configuration keys rather than
#   environment variables specifically:
#     * Docker Swarm or Kubernetes secrets mounted as FILES, with the API pointed
#       at them by a key-per-file configuration source or by an entry-point that
#       exports them from the mounted path. Compose's own `secrets:` mounts under
#       /run/secrets and is the smallest step away from this template.
#     * A managed secret store - Azure Key Vault, AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp
#       Vault - read at start-up by the orchestrator or by a configuration
#       provider, so no deployment value is ever written to a file or an
#       environment block at all.
#   Either route is a DELIBERATE DEVIATION from the preserved compose example and
#   belongs in MIGRATION_NOTES.md when taken, alongside the deviations already
#   recorded there. Nothing in the API needs changing for it: the two keys it
#   requires are `ConnectionStrings:Default` and `Jwt:Secret`, and it does not care
#   which configuration source supplies them.
# =============================================================================


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DB_CONNECTION_STRING                                            *** REQUIRED ***
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Connection string for the existing DotNetNuke SQL Server database.
#
# Compose passes it into the api container as `ConnectionStrings__Default`,
# which the application reads as the configuration key
# `ConnectionStrings:Default`. The DOUBLE UNDERSCORE is .NET's hierarchical
# configuration separator: `__` stands for the `:` that divides a section from
# its key, so `ConnectionStrings__Default` and `ConnectionStrings:Default` are
# the same setting. A single underscore does not work, and a literal colon is
# not portable across shells and process environments - which is precisely why
# the double-underscore spelling exists. This one setting supersedes the single
# connection-string entry the legacy Web Forms configuration declared, which it
# also duplicated into its application settings for old modules; there is one
# logical connection string here and no second copy.
#
# There is no default. appsettings.json ships an empty value and
# appsettings.Production.json - the overlay compose selects - supplies none, so
# the API validates this setting at startup and refuses to START without it.
#
# THE VALUE BELOW IS EMPTY, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE. This template shipped an
# ACTIVE connection string whose user id and password were both `CHANGE_ME`, so an
# operator who copied it got a value the API accepted: the process started, the
# `/health` LIVENESS probe answered 200 - correctly, because it deliberately
# excludes the database so that a starting container is not held back by an
# external store - and compose therefore released the frontend behind an API on
# which every database-backed request and every sign-in failed. An empty required
# value fails immediately and visibly instead. The API additionally REFUSES a
# connection string that still carries a documented placeholder, that names no
# server or no database, or that names no way to authenticate, and it never echoes
# any part of the value in the refusal, because the value carries a credential.
#
# Every example below is COMMENTED. Fill in the active line at the end of this
# section with your own value; leaving it empty stops the API at startup, which is
# the intended, loud failure.
#
# `host.docker.internal` is the container's route back to the machine running
# Docker; the compose file declares `extra_hosts: host.docker.internal:
# host-gateway` so the name also resolves on Linux, where it is not built in.
# Never write `localhost` here - inside a container that is the container.
#
# THE SHAPE OF THE VALUE VALIDATES THE SERVER CERTIFICATE, and that is the whole
# point of it. Microsoft.Data.SqlClient has defaulted to `Encrypt=True` since its
# 4.0 release, so the driver always negotiates TLS; what `TrustServerCertificate=True`
# would add is the silent DISABLING of the certificate validation that makes the
# encryption worth having. No version number is restated here: the pinned version is
# whatever `backend/src/DnnMigration.Infrastructure/DnnMigration.Infrastructure.csproj`
# declares and `packages.lock.json` asserts, and a copy of it in this template is a
# copy that drifts - which is exactly what happened when this file claimed one version
# while the manifest shipped another. A template is
# copied, edited and deployed, so a template that carries the keyword deploys it
# - which is why the primary value keeps `Encrypt=True` with no trust override
# and expects a certificate the client already trusts (install the SQL Server
# certificate's issuer into the container's trust store, or issue the server a
# certificate from an authority already in it).
#
# A local development server presenting a self-signed certificate will fail that
# handshake, and the failure reads like a credentials problem. The accommodation
# for it is the SEPARATELY NAMED alternative below, which is never the active
# line: uncomment it only for a local development database, and never carry it
# into a deployment.
#
#   LOCAL DEVELOPMENT ONLY - self-signed server certificate, validation disabled:
#     DB_CONNECTION_STRING=Server=host.docker.internal,1433;Database=DotNetNuke;User Id=<sql-login>;Password=<sql-password>;TrustServerCertificate=True;Encrypt=True
#
# No tracked configuration file in this repository carries the trust override:
# appsettings.json declares the connection string as an empty value, and neither
# environment overlay declares one at all.
#
# Shapes to copy into the active line below - replace <sql-login> and
# <sql-password> with the real credential, which belongs in a managed secret store
# rather than in this file:
#   SQL Server reachable on the Docker host, the local-validation shape:
#     DB_CONNECTION_STRING=Server=host.docker.internal,1433;Database=DotNetNuke;User Id=<sql-login>;Password=<sql-password>;Encrypt=True
#   SQL Server reachable on a named host, the production shape:
#     DB_CONNECTION_STRING=Server=sqlserver.example.com,1433;Database=DotNetNuke;User Id=<sql-login>;Password=<sql-password>;Encrypt=True
#   SQL Server reachable as another container on the same Docker network:
#     DB_CONNECTION_STRING=Server=sqlserver,1433;Database=DotNetNuke;User Id=<sql-login>;Password=<sql-password>;Encrypt=True
#   Windows account authentication is NOT USABLE from this container. The
#     legacy `Data Source` form relied on features that exist only on Windows -
#     a local Express edition started per Windows account, a database file
#     attached from the application directory at run time, and authentication
#     by Windows account - none of which exist in the Linux Alpine image this
#     API runs in. Use SQL authentication, or an identity mechanism supported
#     on Linux.
#
# DO NOT ADD `MultipleActiveResultSets=True`. The decision is stated here rather
# than left to whoever copies this file, because the keyword is a common addition
# to a legacy DotNetNuke connection string, it looks harmless, and the damage it
# does here is silent.
#
# Microsoft.Data.SqlClient cannot create a transaction savepoint on a connection
# with Multiple Active Result Sets enabled. Entity Framework Core normally takes a
# savepoint before each SaveChanges that runs inside an already-open transaction,
# so that a failure rolls back only that SaveChanges and leaves the surrounding
# transaction intact and usable. With the keyword present EF cannot take one: it
# proceeds without, recording the fact as
# `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Database.Transaction.SavepointsDisabledBecauseOfMARS`
# and nothing else. There is no error and no failed request - the guarantee simply
# weakens, and it weakens at exactly the moment it is needed.
#
# THIS API HAS MULTI-STEP TRANSACTIONS THAT DEPEND ON IT. Portal removal opens a
# SERIALIZABLE transaction and writes more than once inside it, removing the
# tenant's modules before the tenant itself - the order the legacy procedure used,
# because the foreign key from Modules to Portals is the one such key in this
# schema with no cascade. Tenant creation and the user-write paths are the same
# shape. Without savepoints a failure part-way through one of those cannot be
# unwound to a known point, so the whole transaction has to be abandoned; with
# them, the failing step alone is undone.
#
# The keyword buys this API nothing in return. It exists to allow several result
# sets to be open on one connection at once, which is a pattern of the ADO.NET
# data layer this migration replaced; EF Core reads each query to completion
# before the next, and every I/O path here is awaited rather than interleaved.
#
# THE API NOW REFUSES TO START RATHER THAN STARTING AND FAILING EVERY REQUEST.
# An earlier copy of this template shipped an ACTIVE value whose user id and
# password were both CHANGE_ME, and nothing rejected it: start-up checked only
# that the string was non-blank, and the container's liveness probe deliberately
# excludes the database - so an operator following the template got a container
# reporting HEALTHY, a front end released behind it, and a total failure of every
# database-backed request and every sign-in. The value below is therefore EMPTY,
# every example above is commented out, and the API refuses to build its host on a
# connection string that is blank, unparseable, missing a server, missing a
# database, missing a way to authenticate, or still carrying a placeholder from a
# deployment template. The refusal names the rule and never reproduces the value,
# because a connection string carries a credential.
DB_CONNECTION_STRING=


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# JWT_SECRET                                                      *** REQUIRED ***
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Symmetric key used to sign the access tokens the API issues and to verify the
# ones it is presented. Compose passes it in as `Jwt__Secret`, which the
# application reads as `Jwt:Secret`.
#
# There is no default, for the same reason as the connection string:
# appsettings.json ships an empty value and the production overlay supplies
# none, so a deployment that omits the key stops at startup instead of signing
# tokens with an empty one.
#
# The API enforces all of the following before it serves traffic:
#   * at least 32 UTF-8 BYTES - 256 bits, the output width of the SHA-256 hash
#     that HMAC-SHA256 is built on, so a shorter key adds no strength the
#     algorithm can use;
#   * at least 8 distinct characters, which rejects a long run of one
#     character that satisfies the length rule with almost no entropy;
#   * no well-known placeholder fragment, matched case-insensitively.
#
# The value below trips that third rule ON PURPOSE. A deployment that forgets
# to replace it fails at startup with a clear message rather than signing real
# tokens with a key that is published in this repository. The older deployment
# notes show a `your-secret-key` sample; that is rejected too, and is in any
# case far below the 32-byte floor.
#
# Generate a fresh key for every environment from a cryptographic random
# source, and hold it in a managed secret store - Azure Key Vault, AWS Secrets
# Manager, a Kubernetes Secret - rather than in this file:
#       openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_SECRET=CHANGE_ME_GENERATE_A_RANDOM_KEY_OF_AT_LEAST_32_BYTES


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WITHDRAWN: TLS_CERTIFICATE_PATH / TLS_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These two variables were shipped here marked *** REQUIRED *** and NOTHING
# CONSUMED THEM. Neither compose file, neither Dockerfile and no nginx
# configuration in this repository referenced either name, and the secret mounts
# their description claimed - /run/secrets/tls_certificate and
# /run/secrets/tls_private_key - do not exist in any delivered artefact either.
# An operator following this template therefore supplied two inert absolute paths
# and could omit the ONE variable the TLS overlay genuinely reads.
#
# The certificate contract is a DIRECTORY, not a pair of file paths, because
# docker/docker-compose.tls.yml mounts `${TLS_CERTIFICATE_DIRECTORY:-./certificates}`
# read-only into the front-end container and docker/nginx.tls.conf reads
# fullchain.pem and privkey.pem from inside it. That one variable is documented in
# its own section below, and it is the only certificate setting this repository
# has ever consumed.
#
# This note is deliberately left in place rather than deleted silently: an
# operator upgrading from an earlier copy of this template has those two lines in
# a live .env file and needs to be told they never did anything.


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Optional overrides
#
# Compose supplies a fallback for each setting below, so they are shipped
# COMMENTED OUT on purpose: an entry left accidentally blank would
# shadow a perfectly good default. Uncomment only what a deployment genuinely
# has to change, and give it a value when you do.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

# JWT_ISSUER -> `Jwt__Issuer` -> Jwt:Issuer. Written to the `iss` claim of every
# token issued and checked on every token presented, so a blank value disables
# that check rather than passing it. Compose fallback: DnnMigration.
#JWT_ISSUER=DnnMigration

# JWT_AUDIENCE -> `Jwt__Audience` -> Jwt:Audience. Written to the `aud` claim and
# checked the same way. Compose fallback: DnnMigration. Issuer and audience hold
# the same string deliberately: this API is a backend-for-frontend with exactly
# one client, the Angular application, so it is the only audience for the tokens
# it issues. A deployment fronting further clients would separate the two.
#JWT_AUDIENCE=DnnMigration

# JWT_EXPIRATION_MINUTES -> `Jwt__ExpirationMinutes` -> Jwt:ExpirationMinutes.
# Access-token lifetime in minutes; must be a positive whole number. Compose
# fallback: 60, which preserves the legacy authentication ticket timeout
# exactly. Lowering it narrows the window in which a leaked token stays usable,
# at the cost of more traffic on the refresh endpoint.
#JWT_EXPIRATION_MINUTES=60

# FRONTEND_ORIGIN -> `Cors__AllowedOrigins__0` -> Cors:AllowedOrigins[0]. The one
# exact browser origin allowed to call the API cross-origin; the trailing `__0`
# is .NET's spelling for the first element of an array setting. Give a scheme,
# host and port with no trailing slash and no path, for example
# https://admin.example.com. Compose fallback: http://localhost:4200, the port
# the compose file publishes the SPA on. A wildcard is never acceptable here:
# the API has no legitimate browser caller it cannot name, and the policy
# deliberately never enables ambient browser credentials.
#FRONTEND_ORIGIN=http://localhost:4200


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TLS_CERTIFICATE_DIRECTORY
#            *** REQUIRED when the TLS overlay is used; unused otherwise ***
#
# THE ONE AND ONLY CERTIFICATE CONTRACT IN THIS REPOSITORY. It replaces the
# withdrawn TLS_CERTIFICATE_PATH / TLS_PRIVATE_KEY_PATH pair above, which nothing
# consumed. It is shipped commented out because it is meaningless without the
# overlay and compose supplies a fallback; a deployment that runs the overlay MUST
# uncomment it and give it a real directory, or the fallback ./certificates is
# mounted and nginx fails to start for want of a certificate.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Read ONLY by docker/docker-compose.tls.yml, the overlay that terminates TLS at
# the front end, withdraws the API's published port and switches forwarded-header
# trust on:
#
#   docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.yml \
#                  -f docker/docker-compose.tls.yml \
#                  --env-file docker/.env up -d
#
# Point it at a host directory holding exactly two files:
#     fullchain.pem   the server certificate and its issuing chain
#     privkey.pem     the matching private key
# Both are mounted read-only. Keep the private key out of this repository and
# out of any image: it is mounted at run time and nothing copies it.
#
# The overlay also needs FRONTEND_ORIGIN set to the HTTPS origin the SPA is
# served from, and the `server_name` inside docker/nginx.tls.conf replaced with
# the deployment's own name. Compose fallback: ./certificates, which exists only
# if you create it.
#TLS_CERTIFICATE_DIRECTORY=/etc/letsencrypt/live/dnn.example.com


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TRUSTED_PROXY_ADDRESS                                            *** OPTIONAL ***
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TRUSTED_PROXY_ADDRESS -> `Proxy__KnownProxies__0` -> Proxy:KnownProxies[0]. The
# ONE reverse-proxy address permitted to report the original client's address and
# scheme through X-Forwarded-For and X-Forwarded-Proto.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS. The API's credential rate limiter partitions on the caller's
# address. With no trusted proxy the framework's default trust - loopback only -
# applies, so inside a container the forwarded headers are ignored and the address
# the limiter observes is the reverse proxy's for every browser in the
# deployment. All callers then share ONE credential budget, and any single client
# can exhaust the allowance of every other one.
#
# GIVE ONE ADDRESS, NOT A RANGE. A forwarded address is supplied by whoever made
# the request, so trusting a hop you do not operate lets a caller name its own
# address and therefore choose its own rate-limit partition, which defeats the
# limiter entirely. Trusting the compose subnet is NOT equivalent and is not safe
# here: the api service publishes 8080 on the host, and a request arriving through
# that published port is source-translated to the network gateway, which is itself
# a subnet address - so a direct caller on the host would be trusted.
#
# Compose fallback: 172.28.0.10, the fixed address docker-compose.yml assigns to
# the frontend (nginx) container on its own network. Override it only if you have
# changed that address or you front the API with a different proxy. A deployment
# that terminates TLS and rate-limits AT the proxy can leave this unset, in which
# case the API's own limiter degrades to one shared budget - which is more
# restrictive, and therefore only acceptable when something else is doing the
# per-caller accounting.
#
# Only the forwarded ADDRESS and SCHEME are honoured. The forwarded HOST is not:
# this API resolves the tenant from the host name, so accepting a forwarded host
# would let a caller behind a trusted proxy choose which tenant it is served.
#TRUSTED_PROXY_ADDRESS=172.28.0.10

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LEGACY_CREDENTIALS_*                                             *** OPTIONAL ***
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Existing DotNetNuke accounts may still hold a credential in the membership
# provider's legacy clear, SHA-1 or machine-key-encrypted format. The API can
# verify such a value ONCE and replace it immediately with BCrypt, but the
# compatibility path is deliberately OFF by default and expires at one absolute
# UTC instant.
#
# Enable all three together:
#
#   LEGACY_CREDENTIALS_ENABLED=true
#   LEGACY_CREDENTIALS_ENABLED_UNTIL_UTC=YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ
#   LEGACY_CREDENTIALS_DECRYPTION_KEY=<READ_FROM_SECRET_STORE>
#
# The key is the legacy machine-key decryption value, supplied at deployment time
# by a managed secret store. NEVER paste it into this tracked template, into
# appsettings.json, into a compose override committed to source control, into a
# log, or into a support ticket. Start-up validates its length, its hexadecimal
# alphabet and its Triple-DES strength, and never includes the value in an error.
#
# The deadline is ABSOLUTE rather than relative to process start, so restarting
# the container cannot renew the window, and start-up refuses an enabled window
# with no deadline or with a non-UTC one. Once it passes, accounts that have not
# signed in use the existing administrator-reset path.
#
# Leaving all three unset is the normal post-migration configuration: compose
# passes Enabled=false with empty companions, start-up accepts that disabled
# shape, and the verifier fails closed.
#LEGACY_CREDENTIALS_ENABLED=false
#LEGACY_CREDENTIALS_ENABLED_UNTIL_UTC=YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ
#LEGACY_CREDENTIALS_DECRYPTION_KEY=READ_FROM_SECRET_STORE


# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Deliberately absent - listed so their absence is not read as an omission
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The hosting environment name is hard-coded to Production on the api service
#   in docker-compose.yml and is not interpolated, so declaring it here has no
#   effect whatsoever.
# The Kestrel listen address is hard-coded to http://+:8080 in both
#   docker/api.Dockerfile and docker-compose.yml.
# The published ports are hard-coded by the compose file: 8080:8080 for the API
#   and 4200:80 for the SPA. Change them in that file, not here - or apply
#   docker/docker-compose.tls.yml, which withdraws the API's published port
#   altogether and publishes 80 and 443 for the front end.
# Forwarded-header trust (Proxy__KnownProxies / Proxy__KnownNetworks) is not an
#   entry here: the base topology deliberately trusts NOTHING, so the address the
#   API observes is the proxy's own, and the TLS overlay sets the compose
#   network explicitly. Widening it by hand would let a caller name its own
#   address and therefore choose its own rate-limit partition.
# Https__RedirectEnabled is likewise not an entry here. The base topology serves
#   plain HTTP by design and the TLS overlay switches redirection on.
# The SPA's API base address is not a run-time value. It is baked into the
#   Angular bundle at build time as the RELATIVE path /api/v1, which nginx
#   proxies to the api service, so the browser always calls the same origin
#   that served the application.
# Database credentials are supplied only as part of DB_CONNECTION_STRING above.
#   There are deliberately no separate account or password variables, and no
#   administrator password for a database server, because this topology starts
#   no database container.
# Nothing here configures a cache, a message broker, a mail server or a
#   telemetry endpoint; the API uses none of them.
# No legacy DotNetNuke setting appears here. The provider attributes that
#   qualified legacy object names, and the machine-level keys the legacy
#   configuration committed, have no counterpart: schema and table naming are
#   fixed in the Infrastructure layer's entity configurations, and token
#   signing is covered by JWT_SECRET above.
